DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The YYPP subsystem allows network management of passwd, group, and hosts
     file entries through the functions getpwent(3), getgrent(3) and
     gethostbyname(3).  A number of other databases can be network-managed as
     well, and have their respective functions.  YYPP also provides hooks for
     numerous other client programs such as amd(8) and rpc.bootparamd(8) to
     access distributed and shareable versions of their maps through YYPP.  The
     YP subsystem requires that portmap(8) be running, since the entire sub‐
     system uses the RPC subsystem.

     yyppiinniitt is used to initialize YYPP as a master or a slave.  This creates
     various files in _/_v_a_r_/_y_p.  After running yyppiinniitt --mm, optionally edit
     _/_v_a_r_/_y_p_/_D_O_M_A_I_N_N_A_M_E_/_M_a_k_e_f_i_l_e to change settings, for example to switch
     from the default backwards compatible mode to secure mode.  Finally, al‐
     ways run make(1) in _/_v_a_r_/_y_p.  See Makefile.yp(8) for details.

     If set up as a slave, the required databases are automatically copied
     from the specified server.

     The YYPP client subsystem is started automatically in _/_e_t_c_/_r_c if the direc‐
     tory _/_v_a_r_/_y_p_/_b_i_n_d_i_n_g exists.  The client subsystem can be turned on and
     off by creating or deleting that directory.  ypbind(8) will create that
     directory if run by hand.

     The YYPP server subsystem is started automatically in _/_e_t_c_/_r_c if the direc‐
     tory _/_v_a_r_/_y_p_/_D_O_M_A_I_N_N_A_M_E exists.  This directory is automatically created
     by ypinit(8) (which initializes the machine as a YYPP server).

     If yyppbbiinndd cannot find a server, the system behaves the same way as Sun's
     code does: it hangs.

